Also, succubi/incubi will remove certain cursed items.

>9:  If you eat a tin of deep-fried monster meat, your rings, gloves, and 
>    weapon will fall on the floor even if these are cursed.

Not true (fixed long ago, unless HARD isn't defined)

>Note that there is approximately a 95% chance of losing a ring if you drop
>it down a sink.  However, if you kick the sink enough (ctrl-d) you have
>some chance of getting the ring back.  ...

Not true.  You'll get _a_ ring, but it's probably not going to be the one you
put in.

>"You hear the wailing of Caa-awn."+            - character about to die
>   + Actually, I think it may be one of several names here.

Wailing of the Banshee: when you have 1 hit point left
Howling of the CwnAnnwn: when you have >1 hit point left
(Messages replaced with Gauntlet messages for Elf, Valkyrie, Wizard)

>"You hear a rushing sound."(possibly an air elemental)

A rushing noise comes from an elemental or similar swallower that missed you
because you were displaced or invisible.

>Cursed and blessed wands function no differently from ordinary wands.

They have a small chance of exploding.

