A few remarks:
Demonbane, Giantslayer, and Werebane should be obvious as to what their effects
are.  (slay demons, giants, and lycanthropes).  The bonus is _not_ 5 and 0
(for instance); a bonus is a number means a random value from 1-number, and 0
means double damage if used for damage.  Sting warns only against orcs.
Sunsword does extra damage to undead.

The attack which Excalibur defends against is level drain.  (Defender is really
a misnomer, which people say only because of SPFX_DEFN appearing in the code.
It's not really a defender, as in AD&D or Moria, at all.)

>| lump of royal jelly |   0 | 1 | 200 | Increases strength          |

And heals and fixes bad legs.

>| slice of pizza      |  75 | 1 | 250 |                             |
>| slime mold          |  75 | 1 | 250 |                             |

Slices of pizza no longer exist after slime molds were added.

>| gelatinous cube  |  10 |  100 |                                            |
>| quivering blob   |  10 |  100 |                                            |

Many intrinsics are possible.

>| quantum mechanic |  20 |  200 | toggles speed (fast/normal), aggr monsters |

Doesn't aggravate monsters.

>| zombie           |   3 |   30 |                                            |

Depends on corpse of whatever kind of zombie it was.  (Same for mummies).
>Extra healing
>   Reg. - Heals you (or adds 4 points to max. if at max.)

2 points.

>Object detection
>   Reg. - You are shown where objects are on level.
>          "You sense the presence of objects."
>          "You have a strange feeling." (if no objects on level)

Cursed: any mimics disguised as objects know where you are.

>Sickness
>   Cur. - Stats lost and sickness.
>   Reg. - You become sick.
>          "Yech!  This stuff tastes like poison."
>   Bls. - No effect.

No: Cursed and regular both cause you to lose a stat and take damage.  If
you're poison resistant, the stat only goes down by 1 point, and if the potion
is cursed you take more damage.  If blessed you take 1 hp of damage.

>RING EFFECTS:
>   Note that all rings increase your food consumptions to varying degrees.

+0 rings don't.

>Shock resistance 
>   No more "Died of System Shock."  Useful when polymorphing. 

Sigh.  Will rumors never cease?  Shock resistance has absolutely nothing to do
with system shock.  Shock resistance protects against electrical damage.
To protect against system shock, wear a ring of polymorph control.

>Create monster
>   Cnf. - Creates many monsters.

Creates many _acid_blobs_ (not monsters in general) if confused.

>Destroy armor (If no armor, "You have a strange feeling.")

Cursed: won't destroy cursed armor, but instead vibrates it, which damages you.

>Food detection
>   Cnf. - Detects all food on the level.

No, detects all _potions_ on the level if read while confused.

>Gold detection
>   Cnf. - Detects traps.
>   Cur. - Incorrect map.
>   C+C  - The map shows you the traps, but makes them look like gold.

Wrong.  The C+C effect above also applies to just plain cursed scrolls.

>Remove curse
>   Reg. - Removes curses and stickeycurses on worn items.

"Stickeycursed" is a term from an earlier spoiler list which referred to you
being unable to remove/unwield cursed items.  This is really the _only_ type of
curse there is, and shouldn't be given a different name.

>Scare monster
>   Reg. - Should be dropped and stood upon.
>          "You hear maniacal laughter in the distance."
>   Cnf. - No effect.
>          "You hear a sad wailing in the distance."

Aggravates monsters.
